ment Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":309,"text":"green banking products. The Bank integrates ESG aspects\nits business, carries out its operations and provides\n2025-26 वाार्षिVिक रिRपाेे(र्टट Annual Report\nin credit appraisal, the Bank has ESG scorecards for listed\n2) Recruitment Drive\nentities to assess the ESG aspects of the borrowers.\nThe Bank has inducted 1801 employees (POs – 319, Specialized officers – 268, Clerk -1208, Sub-staff – 6) during FY26.\nGreen finance is the focus area of the Bank. Accordingly,\nRenewable Energy & Electric Vehicle are the two sectors\n3) Welfare measures for SC/ST/OBC/PWD employees\nidentified as Champion sector for credit focus in the Bank.\n• As per Government of India guidelines, reservation is provided to Scheduled Castes (SCs), Scheduled Tribes (STs), Other Backward Classes (OBCs), Economically Weaker Sections (EWS), Persons with Benchmark Disabilities (PwBDs) and Ex- Servicemen (ESM) candidates in Direct Recruitment.\nThe Bank is raising green deposits and deployment of the\nsame is towards green activities/projects in line with RBI\ndirections. In addition to the green finance eligible sectors\nas per RBI directions, Bank focuses on Battery Energy\nStorage Systems (BESS) and Green Hydrogen sector for\nGreen Finance. Green finance portfolio of the Bank has\nincreased by 61% during FY26.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c695","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":310,"text":"increased by 61% during FY26.\n• Reservation in promotion is provided for SCs, STs and PwBDs employees from Sub-Staff to Clerical cadre & Clerical cadre to Officer in JMG Scale-I as per Government guidelines.\nBank became a member of the Partnership for Carbon Accounting Financials (PCAF), gaining access to expert guidance for estimating financed emissions and aligning its portfolio with net-zero objectives. The Bank has estimated the financed emissions of corporate loan portfolio using PCAF methodology.\n• The Bank has a Chief Liaison Officer for SC/ST/PwBD/ ESM and a Chief Liaison Officer for OBC/EWS in the rank of General Manager to redress the grievances (if any) of SC/ST/OBC/ EWS/PwBD/ESM employees. A separate dedicated Cell is functioning at Corporate Office, HRM Department to look after the grievances\nBank has adopted Policy on Green Building and Sustainability Practices for sustainable management of its own operations. Bank also assessed the vulnerability of physical climate risk events to all its branches and offices.\nof SC/ST/OBC/ EWS/PwBD/ESM employees.\nBasel III Capital Regulations:\n4) HR Initiatives during the year:","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c696","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":310,"text":"4) HR Initiatives during the year:\nThe Basel III Capital Regulations became effective in India since 1st April, 2013 and fully implemented since 1st October, 2021. Common Equity Tier I Capital of the Bank is 16.40%, Total Tier I at 16.40% and Total Capital Adequacy Ratio stands at 17.93% as on 31.03.26. Bank has adequate headroom to raise capital in all forms in case of need. The Basel III capital rules also require an enhanced set of\n• EmoHero Initiative – First line of emotional support\nfor employees, through digital PRACTO platform\nhelping to create a more connected and empathetic\nworkplace.\n• Micro-Learning Unified Platform – Host Gamified\nquizzes for all departments, facilitates Badges,\ndisclosures on the components of Capital Adequacy Ratio\nCertificates and Peer-Peer recognition dashboard.\n(CAR) which are published on quarterly basis on Bank’s\nOver 2.5 Lakh participations registered.\nwebsite. Bank is also disclosing leverage ratio, Liquidity\n• Health Care – Tie up with M/s Practo for free online\nCoverage Ratio (LCR) and Net Stable Funding Ratio (NSFR).\nDoctor consultation for serving and retired staff\nA holistic capital conservation module is developed by the\nmembers: 44818 enrolled\nBank for monitoring and taking corrective action towards\n• Enhancement of Staff Housing Loan Ceilings and\ncapital conservation.\nreduction in interest rate for Staff members","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c697","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":310,"text":"reduction in interest rate for Staff members\n14. HUMAN RESOURCE MANAGEMENT 1) Manpower Position (Domestic) The position of manpower in the Bank as on 31.03.26 is as follows:\n• Enhancement of Residential Accommodation ceiling\nfor officers\n15. HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT\n• 1-day Rashtriya Karmayogi Training was imparted\nCategory Total OBC SC ST Male Female\nOfficers 25997 8130 4999 1991 18458 7539\nfor 19778 employees of the Bank across all cadres\nClerks 12205 4028 2625 617 7750 4455\nto instill sense of Seva Bhav in line with Capacity\nSub Staff 1761 419 861 113 1531 230\nBuilding Commission of India.\n198 20 155 8 151 47\nFull Time Sweepers*\n• 12-month long Rising Star Leadership Development\nProgram was conducted for 419 identified Officers from Scale IV to Scale VI.\nTotal 40161 12597 8640 2729 27890 12271\n(*Domestic excluding Part Time Sweeper)\n2025-26 वाार्षिVिक रिRपाेे(र्टट Annual Report\n• 1964 unique officers posted in the areas outside their linguistic region were trained in local language to serve customers in their local language, thereby improving customer connect.\nMalaysia, Singapore, Canada, UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia) with effect from 01.09.25. For other countries, customers can contact the Bank's 24x7 helpline (Toll number) at +91 22 4444 2888 for assistance and support.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c698","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":311,"text":"Malaysia, Singapore, Canada, UAE, Qatar, Bahrain, Saudi Arabia) with effect from 01.09.25. For other countries, customers can contact the Bank's 24x7 helpline (Toll number) at +91 22 4444 2888 for assistance and support.\n• APARAJITA Programme -a workshop to initiate the reintegration journey of women in the Bank upon their return from maternity / sabbatical leaves was conducted for 140 women employees.\n4. As per TRAI guidelines, the Bank has launched 140/160 series numbers (140 for Promotional calls & 160 for Transaction Monitoring calls). These numbers can only be used by verified institutions, making it easier for customers to identify genuine calls from the Bank. This helps reduce the risk of scam or phishing calls from random mobile numbers. It also builds customer trust, improves the chances of important calls being answered, and ensures compliance with regulatory standards.\n• Advanced Credit Management training with duration of 16 weeks was conducted for 63 Officers to create a talent pool in credit. Advanced Forex Management training with duration of 7 weeks was conducted for 57 Officers to create a talent pool in forex business.\n• 9502 employees underwent training in Artificial Intelligence, as part of future ready workforce development.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c699","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":311,"text":"• 9502 employees underwent training in Artificial Intelligence, as part of future ready workforce development.\n5. Conducting Customers’ Meet on August 22,2025: The Bank celebrated its Annual Customers’ Day on August 22,2025, by organizing Customers’ Meet at all the branches worldwide, coinciding with the Bank’s Foundation Day. The occasion served as a platform for customers to share their feedback and expectations, while also fostering direct interaction with the Bank officials to further enhance service delivery.\n• IB EDGILE platform – A data driven training recommendation and monitoring portal was launched, for field leaders to gauge the capacity & competency building requirements of employees under their span.\n• 6089 employees underwent training on Digital Adoption, Mobile Banking and VBX.\n17. INFORMATION SYSTEM SECURITY\n• 976 employees got certified in AML, KYC and Compliance certificate under Bank’s tie-up with IIBF.\nThe Bank believes in providing services to its customers\nin a safe and secured way and prioritizes customer\ndata security without compromising the quality of\n• Workshop for Internal Complaints Committees (PoSH Act 2013) conducted covering all the ICCs of the Bank.\nbanking services. The CIA (Confidentiality, Integrity\nand Availability) is at the heart of architecting the\ncomprehensive information security framework. The","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c700","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":311,"text":"comprehensive information security framework. The\n• 35675 unique employees attended training in the\nBank also emphasizes and implements protection from a\nentire FY26 across all modes of training.\nplethora of hacktivist attempts which spans from phishing\nsimulation, multi-factor authentication, and awareness\n16. CUSTOMER SERVICE\ninitiatives, and provides simple and secure solutions to\n1 Launch of Next Generation Contact Center: In line with our continuous efforts to enhance customer convenience, the Bank has launched Next Generation Contact Center on 01.09.25. The new platforms allow customers to connect with the Bank seamlessly via voice, chat, WhatsApp, email, and social media for a variety of services.\nthe customers.\nThe key elements of the security strategy at the Bank\ninclude:\n• Layered ‘Defense-in-Depth' strategy providing multiple lines of defense across the technology layer.\n• Strong framework of governance in the technology landscape is put in place.\n2. Launch of Easy-to-remember Toll Free Number (TFN): The Bank has introduced a new and easy-to- remember domestic toll-free number 1800 1700 for customer convenience with effect from 01.09.25.\n• Dedicated teams focusing on cybersecurity and fraud risk management are put in place.\n• Working towards zero-trust architecture and state of art security systems.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c701","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":311,"text":"• Working towards zero-trust architecture and state of art security systems.\n3. Dedicated Toll Free Number for NRI Customers: The Bank has launched 24x7 International Toll Free Numbers for NRI customers in 9 countries (USA, UK,\n• 24x7 monitoring and surveillance of systems through\n2025-26 वाार्षिVिक रिRपाेे(र्टट Annual Report\ncybersecurity operation centre.\noption to block digital banking access via reply SMS.\n• Stringent security controls at the time of inducting new applications or servers.\nOnboarding to the Suspect Registry: A centralized\nSuspect Registry is created to compile identifiers of\n• Periodic external security assessments by CERT-In empaneled service provider to ensure confidence in the security controls implemented.\ncybercriminals. The Bank uses this registry to assess risk\nduring onboarding and ongoing customer due diligence.\nThe Registry is integrated with EFRMS, enabling realtime\nblocking of transactions (Internet/ Mobile Banking & UPI)\n• The Bank is certified for ISO 27001:2022 Information Security management system.\nto suspect accounts.\n• The Bank is a critical infrastructure as declared by NCIIPC.\nIntegration with DoT’s MNRL and FRI Systems: The\nBank has integrated with MNRL and FRI where API-based\nintegration allows complete straightthrough processing","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c702","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":312,"text":"integration allows complete straightthrough processing\n• The Bank conducts extensive training and awareness programs for the employees to keep them abreast of the emerging cyber threats and mechanisms to combat with them.\n(STP), including data downloads, digital freezing of\nCustomer Profiles (High/Very High risk), debit freezing,\nmobile number removal from CIFs, and automated\nreporting to DoT.\n• The Bank is enhancing security measures in the view of emerging risks from new AI based applications.\n• MNRL (Mobile Number Revocation List) provides telecomverified lists of permanently disconnected mobile numbers.\n18. INTERNAL CONTROLS\na) INSPECTION\n• FRI (Financial Fraud Risk Indicator) uses AIbased analytics to classify mobile numbers as Medium, High, or Very High risk based on their association with cyber fraud.\n• Bank has launched Dynamic Risk Assessment of Branches for proactive identification, monitoring and mitigation of inherent risks on quarterly basis, based on findings of different audit tools, automated data flow from MIS and integration of various system generated alerts.\nCyber Police Portal Management (CPPM) Cell:\nTo combat cybercrimes, the Ministry of Home Affairs has\nrolled out a dedicated cybercrime reporting portal (www.\n• Audit Employee Assist was launched for real-time on-demand guidance to employees for improved decision making and insights.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c703","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":312,"text":"• Audit Employee Assist was launched for real-time on-demand guidance to employees for improved decision making and insights.\ncybercrime.gov.in) and a helpline number 1930. CPPM Cell\nis working in multiple shifts on 24x7 basis to attend to the\ncomplaints reported in the cybercrime reporting portal.\nb) FRAUD RISK MANAGEMENT:\nThe process of attending alerts on Cyber Police Portal has been automated through API integration of National Cybercrime Reporting Portal (NCRP) with the Bank.\nEnterprise Fraud Risk Management Solution (EFRMS):\nThe Bank has deployed an Enterprise Fraud Risk\nManagement Solution (EFRMS) that performs realtime,","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c704","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":312,"text":"Management Solution (EFRMS) that performs realtime,\nCustomer Awareness Initiatives: • Special Drive for Senior Citizens on Digital Arrest: A Special Drive was initiated where physical meetings have been conducted with Senior Citizens to create awareness on digital arrests and cyber frauds. • Public Awareness Campaigns: The digital media campaign “Khabar Nahi, Khabardar Bano” used short films depicting common fraud scenarios (QR scams, phishing, job scams). These were circulated across social media, the Bank’s website, and digital signage systems. • Ongoing Awareness Drives: SMS, email, and customer meetings are regularly used for educating customers. Cyber Fraud Awareness through regular e-mail, SMS, educational videos on social media, Bank’s website, customer meets etc. was created amongst customers for safe digital transactions to prevent cyber fraud.\npreventive transaction monitoring across all digital\nchannels, including UPI, Internet Banking, IndSMART,\nATM/POS/ECOM, Credit Cards, AEPS and CMS. The\nsystem uses a combination of rulebased detection\nand machine learning models to identify and block\nsuspicious transactions. Machine learning driven adaptive\nauthentication in Internet Banking and IndSMART\nenhances fraud prevention by assigning risk scores and\ntriggering stepup authentication (challenge questions/\nOTP) for highrisk transactions.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c705","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":312,"text":"OTP) for highrisk transactions.\nIVRS Integration with EFRMS: EFRMS is integrated with\nIVRS, enabling realtime automated calls for transactions\nflagged as suspicious. Customers can confirm transaction\nauthenticity or block all digital channels immediately.\nSimultaneously, realtime SMS alerts are sent with an\n2025-26 वाार्षिVिक रिRपाेे(र्टट Annual Report\n19. COMPLIANCE\nby the CGM (For Scale IV & above officers) and\n• In accordance with the Reserve Bank of India guidelines, an independent Compliance Department headed by Chief General Manager as Chief Compliance Officer has been set up in the Bank.\nthe other by GM (For the officers up to scale III\nand award staff). These committees examine\ncases and forward their recommendations to\nCVO/ACVO. While taking final decision in each\ncase CVO/ACVO, considers the advice of the\n• The compliance function ensures strict adherence to all regulations.\nrespective committee.\n• CVO/ACVO provides Case Classification Advice\n• To further strengthen compliance in the Bank, dedicated compliance officer is deployed at every Zone, Field General Manager Office, Overseas Branches and Corporate Office Departments.\n(CCA) either as Vigilance or Non- Vigilance by\nconsidering the facts of the case and based on\nthe broad guidelines provided by CVC.","doc_type":"annual_report","document":"Annual report · FY2026","document_date":null,"document_rank":0,"page_kind":"pdf"},{"id":"c706","section":"Management Discussion & Analysis","tag":"mdna","page":313,"text":"the broad guidelines provided by CVC.\n• Various Compliance Awareness activities like Compliance Conference, Compliance Journal, Compliance Awareness week, Theme based monthly Compliance Quizzes,